  "text_md": "\" Assemble theymselfes in plumpes,\" Supply cacyon of Soulys, sig. F. ii. Rydes into rowte his dede to revenge,\n\nPLOWEFERE. Companion in play. PLOWKKY. Covered with pimples. Forhyme that is smetyne with his awenne\n\nblode, and spredis over alle his lymmes, and waxes plowkky, and brekes owte. MS. Lincoln Med. f. 294.\n\nPresede into the plumpe and with a prynce metes. 3forte Arthui-e, MS. Lincoln, f.\n\nPLOW-LODE. \" Caracuta, plow lode,\" Nominale MS. It seems to be the same as Plough-land, q. v. PLOWMELL. A small wooden hammer occa-\n\n76. When kynge Richard perceved that the people by plumpes fled from hym to Duke Henry.\n\nplumpes fed from hym to Duke Henry.\n\nA pump; Cornw.\n\nPLOWMELL. A small wooden hammer occaNorth in the Midland counties in its stead is used plough-hatchet.\n\nA hard blow.\n\n(5) (6) Directly ; exactly. Var. dial. Forby has plump endicular, perpendicular. PLUM-PORRIDGE. Porridge with plums\n\nin it, a favourite dish at Christmas in some parts of the country. It is mentioned as part of Christmas fare in the Humourist, ed. 1724, p. 22, and by Addison. PLUMP-PATE. A thick-headed\n\nPLOWRING. Weeping. Prompt. Parv.\n\nPLOWSHO. A ploughshare. Kennett.\n\nPLOY. A merry -meeting: North.\n\nPLOYE:. A plough. Nominale MS.\n\nPLUCK. (1) Courage: Var. dial. To pluck up one's heart ; to be bold, to rejoice   Against the pluck, i. e against the inclination.\n\nPLUMP-PATE. thick-headed fellow .\n\nPLUMPY. To churn. Cornw.\n\nPLUMTEN. Plunged. Weber_\n\npudendum. Have at the plum tree seems to have been either the burden of a song or a proverbial phrase. It occurs in Middleton, although Mr. Dyce does not seem to be acquainted with the meaning of the term itself, which may be gathered from Cotgrave, in v. Hoche-prunier, and the Mariage of Witt and Wisdome, p. 16.\n\nto quarrel with him.\n\nTo pluck rose, i. e. to go to the jakes, said of women. Middleton, iv. 222.\n\n(4) i. This same is kind cuckolds luck : These felioweshave given ine a 